# Line endings.
#
# This is a Windows project, so `core.autocrlf=true` is the local default. With
# no attributes file, Git's idea of a file's line endings and the working tree's
# could drift apart, and untouched files kept showing up as modified in
# `git status` while `git diff` showed nothing — phantom changes that added
# whitespace-only noise to every commit.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalizes to LF in the repository *and* checks out LF on
# every platform, so the working tree matches what is stored regardless of each
# machine's autocrlf setting. Nothing here needs CRLF: there are no .bat or
# .cmd files, and Windows tooling reads LF fine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never apply line-ending conversion or attempt a text diff.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.mov binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
